\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A systemd-managed Python http.server (app8080.service) was created on node2, enabled, and confirmed listening on 0.0.0.0:8080. The listening socket and successful HTTP GET (200 OK) were directly observed, including after node2's reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6e7d6f33f2864550\",\"cmd-faf6b491b53c4f52\",\"cmd-8a77b1e4851b4468\",\"global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"WireGuard tunnel from node3 terminated on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"wg0 was configured on node1 (address 10.200.0.1/24, port 51820, peer node3 with allowed-ips 10.200.0.2/32) and committed/saved; node3 was configured as a wg-quick client pointed at node1's WAN IP. `wg show` on both sides confirms interface state and a successful handshake, both initially and after each node's reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ca68a8582e7243f7\",\"cmd-49a756db0c2344a4\",\"cmd-988fcd32c51746c1\",\"cmd-ff972212ece0411d\",\"cmd-c4884377997144d9\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"node3 can reach node2:8080 only through the tunnel terminated on node1, not directly\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Before the tunnel was configured, curl from node3 to node2:8080 failed ('No route to host') because node2's firewalld zone 'node1only' restricts TCP/8080 to source 10.100.47.10/32 (node1's LAN IP) only. Once the tunnel was up, curl succeeded, node2's access log showed the source as node1's IP (proxied via NAT), and node1's conntrack table showed the translation from node3's tunnel IP to node1's LAN IP toward node2 — directly demonstrating the enforced path. Note: the executor achieved this via a firewalld host-based access restriction on the shared provisioned /24 (all three nodes were actually provisioned on one flat 10.100.47.0/24 'mgmt' network, with the topology's separate 'lan' network apparently unused) rather than genuine L2/L3 network segmentation, so 'outside the private network' is enforced logically rather than physically. This is a reasonable and clearly effective substitute given the provisioned topology, but is a deviation worth flagging.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-478c9782857d48a3\",\"cmd-4f45959107fa4e5d\",\"cmd-faf6b491b53c4f52\",\"cmd-7bdc343c55284f64\",\"cmd-5d7b384fe47640f1\"]},{\"id\":\"R4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on all three managed nodes (pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered service/tunnel/data/cluster state)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show a full cycle: pre-reboot boot_id and health baseline, an issued reboot acknowledged, a distinct post-reboot boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed after_executor snapshot for all three nodes), and a working recovered state — node1's wg0/NAT/firewall config intact plus a forced fresh handshake and successful end-to-end curl from node3; node2's app8080.service and firewalld zone active plus a successful end-to-end curl; node3's wg-quick@wg0 active, fresh handshake via ping, and a successful end-to-end curl. A transient post-reboot curl timeout on node1 (cmd-3b6b1ecad9e4452f) is a normal bounded disruption during handshake re-establishment, resolved moments later.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cf98caf40e2d4044\",\"cmd-46abe761a9af4857\",\"cmd-6484d4fe5ca74cac\",\"cmd-6df0259cdae94b4b\",\"cmd-ff972212ece0411d\",\"cmd-0d6153ffc4a046f4\",\"cmd-3d867baaed0a4ab6\",\"cmd-b5b70d7d29134257\",\"cmd-0a687087ea584498\",\"cmd-8a77b1e4851b4468\",\"cmd-395453415b904ffe\",\"cmd-2ddb204ec4b64190\",\"cmd-166bce171ff84b50\",\"cmd-6f6e6883d9bf4328\",\"cmd-c4884377997144d9\",\"cmd-29c7cc852f104fc7\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Two bounded, uncommitted troubleshooting attempts on node1 left no residue: (1) cmd-8bb993b8d40540a9 generated and pending-installed a PKI-bas